a {:href "/venues/223"} "Henley Royal Regatta Course, Henley-on-Thames"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255024"} "15 from 15 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Due to World War II, there had been no recent Canoe Sprint World Championships, which, at the time, was the best predictor of Olympic success in the sport. " [:a {:href "/athletes/10762"} "Gert Fredriksson"] " of Sweden, however, had gained a reputation for his success in smaller European events, and was considered the favorite in both this event and the " [:a {:href "/results/255030"} "K-1 10,000"] ", which he had won the day before. Despite the time that had elapsed, three participants had won medals at the previous Olympics or World Championships: " [:a {:href "/athletes/10371"} "Wim van der Kroft"] " of the Netherlands (bronze in the " [:a {:href "/results/255007"} "1936 K-2 1000"] "), " [:a {:href "/athletes/9625"} "Henri Eberhardt"] " of France (silver in the " [:a {:href "/results/255021"} "1936 Folding K-1 10,000"] "), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10523"} "Czesław Sobieraj"] " of Poland (silver in the 1938 K-1 10,000 Worlds)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In 1948, the opening heats were won by Fredriksson and Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9422"} "Frederik Kobberup Andersen"] ", but the former was clearly the star of the tournament and went on to win this event by a margin of over 6½ seconds ahead of Andersen, who took silver. Eberhardt, meanwhile, battled Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10396"} "Hans Martin Gulbrandsen"] " to the finish line, and captured bronze with only 0.3 seconds to spare."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "47"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10762"} "Gert Fredriksson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img